      "_label": "Description",
      "content": "Western Arrernte watercolourist from Hermannsburg (Ntaria) whose Central Australian landscapes made him one of the best-known Australian artists of the mid-twentieth century."
    },
    {
      "type": "LinguisticObject",
      "classified_as": [
        {
          "type": "Type",
          "_label": "biography statement"
        }
      ],
      "_label": "Biography",
      "content": "Born at the Hermannsburg (Ntaria) Lutheran mission west of Alice Springs, Namatjira worked as a stockman and camel driver before learning watercolour from the visiting painter Rex Battarbee in the mid-1930s. His first solo exhibition, in Melbourne in 1938, sold out, and his luminous views of the MacDonnell Ranges founded what became known as the Hermannsburg School. In 1957 he and his wife Rubina were granted full Australian citizenship, which was then withheld from most Aboriginal people in the Northern Territory. He died in Alice Springs Hospital in 1959."
    }
  ],
